utors say they will  recommend reduced sentences the charges  could carry up to 25 years behind bars  but pina is said to be sentenced in june  coming up on law and crime daily after  days of deliberations jurors reach a  deadlock in ohio  plus far-right website infowars files  for bankruptcy as front man alex jones  faces multiple defamation suits our  legal analysis after the break  [Music]  welcome back a busy weekend for law  enforcement across the country as dozens  of people were injured in three mass  shootings over the easter and passover  weekend two in south carolina in the  third in pittsburgh two teenagers were  killed and at least eight others were  injured after a shooting at a party in  pittsburgh it happened at a home rented  through the short-term rental company  airbnb police say more than 90 shots  rang out early sunday morning at the  party attended by as many as 200 people  this came just a day after someone  opened fire at a busy mall in columbia  south carolina nine people were shot at  the crowded mall saturday and five  others were injured in a stampede during  the gunfire two people have been  arrested in connection to the shooting  marquis robinson and joayne price who is  on house arrest after making bail price  claims he shot his pistol in  self-defense he's been charged with  unlawfully carrying a firearm nine  people were injured in another shooting  sunday at a lounge hosting an easter  bash in south carolina no deaths have  been reported the three mass shootings  are just the latest string of a surge of  gun violence across the country  switching gears now far-right website  infowars files for bankruptcy amongst  admits defamation lawsuits stemming from  alex jones's comments about the sandy  hook school shooting jones the front man  behind the infowars site faces multiple  defamation suits for referring to the  school shooting as a hoax infowars and  two other companies owned by jones have  now filed for chapter 11 bankruptcy  court documents show infowars states it  has assets of zero to fifty thousand  dollars with liabilities of one million  and one dollars to ten million dollars  all this comes as a judge rules jones  will recoup seventy five thousand  dollars originally paid in fines to a  connecticut court jones was initially  fined for not showing up to his  deposition but the money was ordered to  be returned after jones went to a  rescheduled deposition relatives of the  20 children and six educators killed in  the sandy hook massacre sue jones  claiming they've been harassed and have  received death threats from jones  followers anaga's court date is set for  that case to determine how much jones  owes in damages in a separate case out  of texas jones was found liable for  similar damages he's scheduled to be in  court for that case later this month  all right terry kind of just break it  down for us what does filing for  bankruptcy mean for jones in terms of  these civil suits  well jones actually filed for chapter 11  bankruptcy as far as infowars is  concerned and that was filed in texas  chapter 11 basically is putting whatever  pending civil litigation is going on on  hold so it gives the organization a  chance to reorganize essentially but  they can continue to operate and it's  often used by these big corporations to  help them stay in business so they will  pay their creditors back but it's going  to be on a very low percentage of what  was originally owed at least the hope is  that some of the monies will be paid  back but listen there are many  plaintiffs suing for defamation and if  in fact he says he has very little money  the percentage that they get will be  very little yeah and that's kind of what  i want to ask mike about because when we  look at the potential harm and the  damages for calling this massacre a hoax  how high could these damages be and do  we actually see these families  collecting every penny or is it  something else  well it's always difficult with class  action suits because the numbers may be  large when we look at them but when you  look at all the people who are members  of the class that are putting forth the  action  is the money is really not there for  them a lot of the money gets  in these huge cases gets eaten up by the  attorneys and the investigation one of  the things here too is let's not forget  when you file for bankruptcy you should  be very careful because those are  certified and you're swearing to the  truth of the things you're doing so if  people are looking for assets they may  go beyond this bankruptcy to see if this  in fact is a legitimate bankruptcy so he  may not be solving problems and he may  be creating others so it's very  complicated yes the age-old phrase of  it's not the crime it's the cover-up so  you're definitely expecting some of  these people to look into this  bankruptcy great point there mike  when we come back ohio versus william  husel after weeks of testimony and days  of deliberation jurors reach a deadlock  but does that mean a hung jury that's a  head on law and crime daily  [Music]  welcome back to long crime daily jurors  say they are at an impasse as  deliberations continue into day four  monday in the case of former icu  physician william husell an ohio doctor  accused of killing more than a dozen  patients over the course of three years  prosecutors say husso ordered lethal  doses of fentanyl to be administered to  patients from 2015 until 2018 at mount  carmel hospital in columbus ohio he  faces 14 murder charges his defense team  argues whoso was giving end of life  comfort care and not attempting to show  in the lives of his patients originally  the former doctor was charged in the  deaths of 25 patients but 11 of those  charges were dropped earlier this year  over the course of the week's long trial  jurors heard from several people who  worked alongside whoso and the family  members of the several alleged victims  on monday just before a court broke for  lunch the judge read what is called a  howard charge because the jury was  deadlocked the charge instructing the  jury to continue deliberations once they  returned if convicted whosoever faces  the possibility of life in prison jurors  can also consider a lesser offense of  attempted murder for each of the 14  counts the jury was sent home monday  afternoon after a verdict was still not  reached  mike i i don't know what it is but what  do you think the jury is stuck on in  deciding this case that's still having  them deliberate to another day  i think this jury is this jury is stuck  on the fact that there are no  professional standards that were  introduced to show that this doctor went  off the accepted science in his  field of medical care and i think that  really gives a lot of flexibility here  and the law shouldn't be  that flexible where it's so tough to  defend and i think that's really what  the jurors having a problem is at the  end of the day he really didn't put off  the inevitable and i think people are  saying he was aiding people that's an  interesting argument because if he's  convicted that argument could feed into  an appeal of unconstitutionally vague as  to the crime and based on what you're  saying he could win now terry who said  was charged with 14 counts of murder why  do you think the court allowed the  attempted murder charges to also be  added  you know it's very strange to me because  usually when you allow a lesser included  offense it's based on the evidence but  that lesser included offense and the  higher  offense basically have to have the same  element so you have something like a  manslaughter is a lesser included  offense of murder or an assault is a  lesser included offense of rape but here  it's very difficult to understand why  with the original charge of murder the  judge is going to allow the lesser  included of attempted murder because  obviously with attempted murder the  person doesn't die here's my guess i  think the on